{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Make for yourself, etc.</b> It may be explained by way of the prophet Ezekiel's statement (Ezekiel 14:14): ״וְהָיוּ שְׁלֹשֶׁת הָאֲנָשִׁים הָאֵלֶּה בְּתוֹכָהּ נֹחַ דָּנִיֵּאל וְאִיּוֹב הֵמָּה בְצִדְקָתָם יְנַצְּלוּ אֶת נַפְשָׁם״ — meaning: specifically three have the power in their merit to save themselves so that evil does not reach them; but two — and needless to say one — of the three cannot prevent the destroyer from reaching him. And some of our early ones raised a difficulty: do we not find that in the destruction righteous ones and devout ones were killed, as it is written (Psalms 79:2): ״נָתְנוּ אֶת נִבְלַת עֲבָדֶיךָ״? And they resolved it, and this is their language: it is possible that he was a solitary devout man among many at the time of slaughter, and it is said (Psalms 94:15): ״עַד צֶדֶק יָשׁוּב מִשְׁפָּט״; but when they are three or more, their merit combines and they save themselves even within the evil — end quote. This is what HaShem, in the pleasantness of His words, precisely intimated when He commanded him to make an ark to escape within it: He said to him that the reason he needs to save himself in the ark, and his merit will not protect him from the evil drawing near to him, is because there are no righteous men with him and he is found alone — and that is His saying <b>עֲשֵׂה לְךָ</b> in the singular; and therefore he must fear ״עַד צֶדֶק יָשׁוּב מִשְׁפָּט.״ From this you learn that his sons were not righteous like him, for if they had been righteous, in their righteousness they would have saved themselves without any refuge, since they are more than three. And one needs to reconcile the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory that I cited in the preceding verse, that Abraham did not plead for fewer than ten because he learned from Noah, who were fewer than ten, etc. — end quote; and it is difficult: was not Noah only a solitary individual in his generation? And it may be said that they must be completely righteous like Noah, and then they would save themselves after the wrath has gone forth into the world, and the destroyer would not enter their houses to strike; but to protect so that the destroyer does not go forth — even if they are righteous on a lesser level than Noah, they are effective, and even to protect others. And therefore the verse specified in Ezekiel: Noah, Daniel, and Job, and did not say simply ״three righteous men.״ And you should know that once permission has been granted to the destroyer, who is he and what manner is he who can escape him?"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:50.434692277Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}